// Linux process entry and boot sequence for the SEAM 3 remainder (#1608): the
// `_start` stub, the `rtStartMain` stack-walker it tail-calls, and `boot` — the
// Bit form of `runtime/root/`'s `startEntry`/`rtStartMain`/`boot`
// (runtime/root/:1974-2130). This file OWNS the process from the first instruction
// the kernel jumps to until the collector and scheduler are up, the user's
// `bit_main` has run as the first green thread, and the runtime is torn down.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS LIVES IN THE PLATFORM MODULE, NOT PLATFORM-FREE `runtime/root`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `boot` names platform seams a platform-free module may not even DECLARE:
// `mapPages` (raw `mmap`), `threadStart`/`threadRelease` (raw `clone`),
// `parkSleepNs`/`parkMonoNs` (raw `clock_nanosleep`/`clock_gettime`) — all
// `syscall`, which SPEC §11.8 rejects targeting Darwin. So `boot` cannot sit in
// `runtime/root` (that module is emitted for aarch64-macos too) and is instead
// duplicated function-for-function with ../darwin/boot.bit, exactly as
// ./signal.bit is duplicated with ../darwin/signal.bit: a divergence between
// the two is then a real platform difference, not a spelling one. The two boots
// differ in precisely two places — the entry ABI (`_start` reads the raw kernel
// stack; `machoMain` gets C args in registers) and the seam bindings.
//
// THIS MODULE EMITS `--freestanding` (#1628), which costs it two things and is
// worth stating because both look like gratuitous verbosity otherwise:
//
//   - IT BUILDS NO FUNCTION VALUE. A function value is a `(code, env)` cell on
//     the managed heap, so a module that makes one needs a `TypeInfo` descriptor
//     that a freestanding object may not carry (§17.6). Every platform seam here
//     is therefore bound by DIRECT CALL — a local `while` over
//     `schedFindWorkAt`/`pollDrain`/`schedWorkerStepAt` not `schedWorkerRun(w,
//     nowFn, pollFn)`, `schedStartArm` +
//     `threadStart` not `schedStart(..., threadStart)`, `joinWorker` not
//     `schedJoin(..., parkSleepNs, parkMonoNs, threadRelease)`.
//   - EVERY CROSS-MODULE CALLEE IT NAMES IS `@symbol`-PINNED (SPEC §11.9,
//     ABI.md §9), under port-internal `bit_rt_port_*` names: a freestanding
//     object may leave a reference undefined only under a name the callee's own
//     object defines, and a mangled `m<id>$f` is never that name.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AUXV CAPTURE (#1617) AND MAIN-THREAD STATIC-TLS INSTALL (#1608)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/root/`'s `rtStartMain` does two auxv-adjacent things: it CAPTURES the ELF
// auxiliary vector off the initial stack, and it INSTALLS the main thread's
// static TLS from it (`initLinuxTls`: AT_PHDR/AT_PHNUM -> PT_TLS ->
// `tls.initStatic`). #1617 landed the first: `rtStartMain` below walks past
// `envp`'s NULL to the auxv array and stores its address through the single
// sanctioned setter `bit_rt_port_root_os_set_auxv`, into the Bit cell
// `../os.bit`'s `gAuxv` owns. The ABI reader `bit_rt_auxv` (now Bit-provided,
// ABI.md §19) hands it back, so `runtime/auxv`'s `getauxval` and a spawned
// thread's `tlsPrepare` (../../thread/linux/tls.bit) read real auxv data.
//
// #1608 landed the second: `initLinuxTls` below sizes, maps, fills and installs
// the MAIN thread's own static TLS block from that captured auxv, reusing the
// worker path's `tlsAreaBytes`/`tlsPrepare` and a new `setThreadPointer`
// (arch_prctl on x86_64, `msr tpidr_el0` on aarch64 — ../../thread/linux/tls.bit).
// `rtStartMain` calls it after the auxv capture and before `boot`, mirroring
// `runtime/root/` exactly. With that, both the main thread and every worker thread run
// on a correctly-installed thread pointer.
//
// The archive swap (#1369) is what makes THIS the live entry; before it, the
// previous runtime's `rtStartMain`/`g_auxv`/`initLinuxTls` stayed live and this
// path was exercised by freestanding emit + the `getauxval` read-back + the
// TP-set disassembly + the PT_TLS-parse logic isolation, not by a running binary.
//
// Nothing here is `@nosplit`: `boot` allocates (it maps the heap) and runs
// before the stack-guard machinery exists, and `@nosplit` rejects `syscall`
// anyway (E0075). `mainTrampoline` calls the user's `bit_main`, arbitrary work
// that must reach safepoints, so it is not `@nosplit` either.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE BOOT THREAD AND THE RENDEZVOUS (#1660) — kept identical in ../darwin/boot.bit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is the ONE place the Bit boot diverges from `runtime/root/`'s by construction
// rather than by choice, so the reasoning is recorded here in full.
//
// The boot thread never appears in the mutator registry: code
// carries no back-edge safepoint polls, so that thread cannot take a safepoint,
// never calls `currentMutator`, and its `World` slot stays `mFree` — which
// `stoppedFor` counts as stopped. `boot` here is ordinary Bit, so codegen puts a
// `bl bit_rt_safepoint` on step 7's back edge and on `joinWorker`'s. The thread
// therefore DOES claim a slot and DOES go `mRunning`. It then sleeps up to 1 ms
// per iteration while the collector burns its whole `stwSpinBound` (1e6) waiting
// for it, so the rendezvous expires and the collection is abandoned: 165-168
// abandoned collections in a measured 200k-collection `BIT_GC=stress` run.
//
// THE FIX IS NOT "DO NOT REGISTER". A thread that reaches safepoints must be
// visible to the rendezvous for as long as it can run Bit code, and suppressing
// its poll is the exact shape #1650/#1651 had to delete (`schedCurrentTask() == 0`
// suppressed a worker's own OS-thread stack, whose closure cells were then swept
// while live). Nor is it "poll promptly instead of sleeping": that pegs a core
// for the whole program.
//
// It is the ABI's own `blocked` contract (ABI.md §5), applied exactly where
// `runtime/sched/` applies it to its idle sleep: bracket THE SLEEP, and nothing else,
// with `bit_rt_gc_blocking_begin`/`_end`. `blocked` means "holds no live Bit
// reference for the whole bracketed region", and the collector then passes the
// slot without waiting for it and without scanning its stack.
//
// THE PRECONDITION HOLDS, AND IT IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT — a partial root
// enumeration sweeps live objects, so this is not an optimisation to hand-wave.
// `boot` allocates NOTHING on the managed heap, at any point: the heap, the
// scheduler block, the task block, the channel reserve and the stats buffer are
// `mapPages` mappings and module-state arrays (untraced scalars, rule 1), the
// GC config is parsed straight off `environ` as raw pointers
// (`../rootconfig.bit`'s `gcEnvValuePtr`/`gcCstrUsize` build no string), and
// `statsReport` writes packed byte constants into `statsBuf` for the reason its
// own comment gives. Every local live across the bracketed sleep is an `int` or
// a `*i64` into module state, i.e. outside the heap bounds `gcMarkConservative`
// tests. The one managed object this file's control flow ever creates belongs to
// `bit_main`, which runs on the WORKER's green task, not here.
//
// Outside the bracket the thread is `mRunning` and polls normally, which is what
// keeps the registration honest: the running window is the handful of
// instructions between the sleep returning and the loop's back edge, and the
// poll there publishes a real snapshot as any mutator does.

import { mapPages, unmapPages, protectPages } from "../../alloc/linux"
import { threadStart, threadRelease } from "../../thread/linux"
import { tlsAreaBytes, tlsPrepare, tlsUnavailable, setThreadPointer } from "../../thread/linux"
import { schedStartArm } from "../../thread"
import { parkSleepNs } from "../../park/linux"
import { parkMinBackoffNs, parkNextBackoff } from "../../park"
import {
  schedInit, schedTaskInit, schedRegister, schedSpawn, schedShutdown,
  schedWorkerAt, workerStepStop, WORKER_MAX_STEPS, scStopping,
  schedFindWorkAt, schedWorkerStepAt, schedSetPollFd, schedSetPollFdWrite,
  schedTaskExit, dqPushBottom, taskAt, taskWords, schedTaskOnStack,
  schedStackBytes, schedStackAligned, schedRegisterBigStack, wkDeque,
  schedSpawnTrampoline, taskSpawnEntry,
  schedIdleArm, schedIdleDisarm, schedIdlePark, wkId, wkSleepReq,
  wkLastDone, taskStackBase, taskStackBytes,
} from "../../sched"
// The netpoller provider (#1673). `runtime/sched` may not import it — epoll
// against kqueue is the platform split the parent module exists to stay clear of
// — so the binding is made here, in the leaf that is already per-platform, by
// direct call to the three pinned entries. ../darwin/boot.bit binds the kqueue
// twin at exactly the same three points.
import { pollCreate, pollClose, pollDrain } from "../../sched/linux"
import { rootBindMemory, rootInit, rootConfigureFromEnv, rootEnvMarkStack, rootEnvVerbose, rootEnvWorkers, rootStatOom, osSetArgs, osSetAuxv } from "../../root"
import { rootGcAddr, rootWorldAddr } from "../../root"
import { chanBootBind } from "../../chan"
import { gcBlockingBegin, gcBlockingEnd, gcThreadEnter, gcThreadExit, gcDefaultMinTrigger, gcDefaultMarkStack, gcDefaultGrowthPct } from "../../gc"
import { stwBind, stwCollections, stwSwept, stwLiveObjects, stwAbandonedCount } from "../../stw"
import { stwBadOffsets } from "../../stw"
import { stwSpanChunks, stwSpanChunkBytes } from "../../stw"

// `loadWord` (../../gc) and `parkMonoNs` (../../park/linux) are already imported
// by sibling ./io.bit and ./time.bit; siblings share one module scope, so they
// are used here directly — re-importing either is E0042.

// The per-program entry the compiled user object defines (ABI.md §10). Reachable
// as an `extern function` since #1614 whitelisted `bit_main` in the E0078
// archive gate; it resolves to `U bit_main` at final program link, exactly as
// `runtime/root/`'s `extern fn bit_main` does. This is the ONE sanctioned extern in
// the root tree that names no archive-resident symbol.
extern fn bit_main(): i32

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Boot-time storage (single-worker, ABI.md §5)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Sized for `bootMaxWorkers` workers (#1900, ABI.md §9 step 2). All are
// module-level and unlocked, sound because `boot` fills them once on the boot
// thread BEFORE any worker's OS thread exists (the register-before-steal
// precondition `schedStart` documents). Scalar `i64`/`i32` arrays are never
// scanned by the collector (rule 1).
//
// THE SIZES ARE LITERALS AND THE ARITHMETIC IS BY HAND, because an array size
// must be an integer literal — neither `[bootMaxWorkers]i64` nor
// `[workerWords * bootMaxWorkers]i64` parses (E0021). So these cannot be
// derived from the consts they must agree with, and a mismatch would be an
// out-of-bounds write into module state rather than a compile error. There is
// no runtime check of this relationship — keep the literals below in sync by
// hand with bootWorkerWords/bootArgWords/bootMaxWorkers.
//
//   schedBlock     schedWords  = 16
//   workerBlock    workerWords = 292, x32 = 9344
//   mainTaskBlock  one task block = 32 words
let schedBlock: [16]i64
let workerBlock: [9344]i64
let mainTaskBlock: [32]i64

// The three areas `schedStart`/`schedJoin` fill and reap, one entry per worker:
// an `argWords`-word argument block (2 x32 = 64), a done word, a handle slot.
let startArgs: [64]i64
let startDones: [32]i32
let startHandles: [32]i64

// The BOOT THREAD's own `parkSleepNs` scratch (#2318) — never a worker's:
// every worker now carries its own two words (`wkSleepReq`), so this is only
// for the two sleeps the boot thread itself performs (`joinWorker` and
// step 7's poll-wait below), which never run concurrently with each other or
// with any worker.
let bootSleepReq: [2]i64

// The netpoller descriptor `boot` creates, kept so teardown can close it —
// `runtime/sched/`'s `NetPoller.deinit`, which its `Scheduler.deinit` calls. The
// scheduler block holds the same value, but this runtime frees the poller AFTER
// `joinWorker`, at a point where reaching back into a block no thread owns any
// more is a needless coupling. 0 means "never created", which cannot happen past
// step 4b's guard but keeps the close a no-op on any path that skipped it.
let bootPollFd: int = 0

// The write-interest half of the netpoller (#1910), held and closed on exactly
// the same terms as the descriptor above. Linux only: kqueue keys on
// `(fd, filter)` and needs no second descriptor, so ../darwin/boot.bit has no
// twin of this.
let bootPollFdWrite: int = 0

// The main green thread's result, published across the boot thread's poll-wait.
// `mainExitCode` is the process exit code; `mainDone` is the release flag the
// poll loop acquires on — the Bit form of `runtime/root/`'s `MainRun{exit_code,done}`.
let mainExitCode: int = 0
let mainDone: i32 = 0

// `main`'s registered stack base (#2246) — set once, in step 5 of
// `boottail.bit`'s `boot`, right after `mapGuardedStack(mainStackBytes)`
// returns it. `./signal.bit`'s `segvHandler` reads this to tell "main
// overflowed" from "a green thread overflowed" EXACTLY, rather than by
// heuristic: main is the one stack this runtime already registers by address
// (`schedRegisterBigStack`), so there is no reason to guess for it. 0 until
// step 5 runs, which a fault before then (nothing has run any user code yet)
// cannot reach. Identical to ../darwin/boot.bit's.
let mainStackBaseAddr: int = 0

// The base of the `nworkers * sigAltStackBytes` mapping `./signal.bit`'s
// `installAltStackForThisWorker` carves per-worker slices from — set once in
// `boottail.bit`'s `boot`, before any worker OS thread starts. 0 means "no
// alternate signal stack is available", which `installAltStackForThisWorker`
// treats as a no-op rather than a fault, though in practice `boot` returns 70
// first: a process that cannot map 64 KiB per worker is not going to run far
// regardless. Identical to ../darwin/boot.bit's.
let sigAltStackBase: int = 0

// The process-global scheduler address, the Bit form of `runtime/sched/`'s `g_sched`.
// The rest of this runtime is strictly caller-owned — every scheduler path takes
// an explicit `s` — but `bit_rt_spawn(fn_ptr, arg)` carries no scheduler
// argument (ABI.md §10), so the wrapper that implements it must recover the
// scheduler from process-global state, exactly as `runtime/root/`'s
// `g_sched.spawn(fn_ptr, arg)` does. `boot` publishes the block address here
// once `schedInit` has run (via `spawnBindScheduler`); `rtSpawn` reads it.
// Untraced scalar module state (rule 1) — it holds a raw block address, never a
// GC object.
let gSched: int = 0

// The channel reserve descriptor (`reserveWords` = 3) and root-scan registry
// block (`chanRegWords` = 2) `runtime/chan`'s `chan_make` allocates and registers
// through. Owned here — `boot` can `ptrOf` a module array, chan's `@nosplit`/
// freestanding module cannot (E0075) — and their addresses bound into chan once
// at boot via `chanBootBind` (the caller-owned-block pattern). Untraced scalar arrays,
// never scanned (rule 1). Sizes are chan's exported layout widths, hardcoded as
// literals exactly as `schedBlock`/`workerBlock` above are.
let chanReserveDesc: [3]i64
let chanRegBlock: [2]i64

// Heap geometry. 8 MiB object arena + 128 KiB span index, `runtime/root`'s own
// proven bring-up sizes (tests/stress/rootbit). `mapPages` rounds to the true
// page size; 4096 is passed as the nominal page size `rootBindMemory` records,
// matching every other bring-up.
const arenaWords: int = 1048576
const indexWords: int = 16384
const pageSize: int = 4096

// None of the min-trigger, mark-stack or growth-pct defaults is a local const
// (#2920, #2923, #2924): a copy here silently diverged from `runtime/gc/`'s
// `gcDefaultMinTrigger` (64x too low) for two weeks across several releases,
// because nothing compared the two, and `markStack` carried the same shape at
// half of `gcDefaultMarkStack` until #2923 fixed it. `growthPct` matched
// `gcDefaultGrowthPct` today, so #2924 removed the second definition
// pre-emptively rather than fixing live drift. `gcInit` already seeds the
// collector with the real defaults before `boot` ever runs (constraint 7,
// ../../gc/gc.bit); `boottail.bit`'s `rootEnvMarkStack` call and its call to
// `rootConfigureFromEnv` pass those SAME constants through, so "unset" cannot
// mean anything but "leave gcInit's value alone". There is now exactly one
// definition of each default in the tree. Kept identical to ../darwin/boot.bit.

// Worker-pool ceiling (#1900, ABI.md §9 step 2). A STATIC cap, not a dynamic
// allocation, so `boot` maps nothing new for the pool and Power-of-10 rule 3
// still holds: the worker/args/dones/handles blocks above are sized for exactly
// this many and never grow.
//
// 32 covers every machine this toolchain targets today and costs ~75KB of BSS
// (9344 words). A box with more cores gets 32 workers, not an error — the clamp
// is silent on the way down because oversubscribing is the harm, not
// undersubscribing.
const bootMaxWorkers: int = 32

// The literal sizes above, restated for comparison against schedBlock/
// workerBlock/mainTaskBlock's declared lengths — there is no runtime check
// that they agree (see the comment above those declarations).
const bootWorkerWords: int = 292
const bootArgWords: int = 2

// Backing size of the channel reserve `chan_make` bump-allocates handles and
// rings out of: 1 MiB, process-lifetime. v1 has no channel-free primitive
// (ABI.md §11), so this is the process's whole channel budget — a fixed,
// startup-mapped pool (Power-of-10 rule 3: no unbounded allocation after boot),
// generous next to any realistic channel population. `mapPages` rounds to the
// true page size.
const chanReserveBytes: int = 1048576

// Join deadline: generous next to real teardown (a worker leaves `schedWorkerRun`
// microseconds after shutdown is signalled) and finite so a wedge fails rather
// than hangs. Matches the stress harnesses' `WAIT_NS`.
const joinTimeoutNs: int = 15000000000

// Round `addr` up to the next `schedStackBytes` boundary (`schedStackAligned`'s
// contract). One add-and-mask, no allocation.
@nosplit fn alignUpStack(addr: int): int {
  let bumped = addr + schedStackBytes - 1
  return bumped - bumped % schedStackBytes
}

// Round `addr` down to the `schedStackBytes` boundary at or below it — the
// complement of `alignUpStack` above. `./signal.bit`'s `classifyOverflowTrap`
// uses this to find the candidate stack BASE below an arbitrary `sp` reading,
// where `alignUpStack` would instead round UP past it: a SIGSEGV/SIGBUS fault
// address sits BELOW a stack's base (in the guard hole), so rounding up lands
// on the base, but `sp` at a #2432 inline-trap site sits AT OR ABOVE the base
// (the corrupting frame lands ON the self-pointer/tag words, never past them —
// see task.bit's "A task that reaches here"), so rounding up would overshoot
// to the NEXT granule entirely. Identical to ../darwin/boot.bit's.
@nosplit fn alignDownStack(addr: int): int {
  return addr - addr % schedStackBytes
}

// `main`'s own stack, and it is NOT `schedStackBytes` (#1843). `runtime/sched/` gives
// `main` `main_stack_size` = 8 MiB against a goroutine's `stack_size`, because
// `main` is where user code recurses; the port shipped it at 64KiB, which
// `tests/stress/deeprecursion` caught by overflowing into neighbouring module
// state and still printing the right answer. Too large for masked identity, so it
// is named through `schedRegisterBigStack` instead. Identical to the sibling
// platform's.
const mainStackBytes: int = 8388608

// One task stack, aligned as `schedStackAligned` requires and GUARDED BELOW.
// Returns the base (lowest address, where the tag and the chan scratch live), or
// 0 on failure. Identical to ../darwin/boot.bit's.
//
// THE GUARD IS THE POINT, and it costs no extra syscall (#1843). The alignment
// contract already forces mapping extra granules so a correctly-aligned window
// can be carved out of them; that leaves slop BELOW the chosen base which was
// previously left mapped and writable. PROT_NONE-ing it turns it into a hole, so a
// stack that grows past its base faults instead of scribbling over whatever the
// allocator put next. `runtime/root/` had this as `mapGuardedStack`; the port shipped
// without it and `tests/stress/deeprecursion` overflowed ~425KiB into live data
// while still printing the correct answer.
//
// #2433: THE HEAD HOLE IS `protectPages`'d, NOT `unmapPages`'d. It used to be
// unmapped, which frees the address range back to the kernel's free list —
// and `rtSpawn` (../boottail.bit) maps the task control block two statements
// after calling this function. A first-fit `mmap` regularly landed inside the
// hole it had just freed: measured on 40 live spawned tasks, 8 (20%) had NO
// guard left at all, because the task block had consumed the entire hole.
// Making the hole PROT_NONE instead keeps it a live, occupied mapping — still
// inaccessible, so an overflow still faults exactly as before, but no later
// `mapPages` can ever be handed the same address. Identical to ../darwin/boot.bit's.
//
// #2433 (width, this change): the head hole above used to BE `base -
// region` — a stated maximum of one granule, but no floor: whatever the
// alignment carve left, which could be as little as a single OS page
// (4 KiB on Linux/x86, 16 KiB on aarch64-macOS). One oversized frame is
// then enough to step clean over the hole into whatever mapping follows it,
// silently, instead of faulting. The head hole is now FLOORED to exactly
// `schedStackBytes` (64 KiB) below `base`, every call, regardless of where
// the allocator's alignment landed `region`. `schedStackBytes` is the floor
// rather than an arbitrary new constant because it was already this hole's
// observed MAXIMUM before this change — the alignment carve never left
// more — so the floor never makes a guard narrower than the strongest case
// this function already produced by luck, only removes the luck, and it
// keeps every distance in this file in the one granule the masked-identity
// scheme already uses. It costs 64 KiB of reserved, PROT_NONE VIRTUAL
// ADDRESS SPACE per spawned task — never resident, since PROT_NONE pages
// are never faulted in. Identical to ../darwin/boot.bit's.
//
// To guarantee a full granule below `base` regardless of alignment, the
// mapped region is now `bytes + 2 * schedStackBytes` (one granule wider
// than before) and `base = alignUpStack(region + schedStackBytes)` rather
// than `alignUpStack(region + 1)`, forcing `base` at least one whole
// granule above `region` so `[base - schedStackBytes, base)` is always
// entirely inside the mapping. The leftover below that fixed guard (0 to
// `schedStackBytes - 1` bytes, still alignment-dependent) is freed via
// `unmapPages`: safe, because it sits further from `base` than the guard,
// so a downward overflow always reaches the fixed guard first, regardless
// of what a later allocation does with the freed sliver.
//
// THE TAIL HOLE (#2167 follow-up) is unrelated to downward-overflow
// detection — the stack grows DOWN from `base + bytes`
// (`schedTaskContext`'s call site below) — but is sized off the same
// mapping and moves with it: the freed head sliver plus the fixed head
// guard together take `schedStackBytes` of the `bytes + 2 * schedStackBytes`
// total (by construction), so what remains above `base` is `bytes` plus a
// tail of `schedStackBytes - headSlop` bytes — never touched, never shows
// up as RSS, but still a live, counted VMA for as long as the process runs,
// one per call. `rtSpawn` calls this once per `spawn`, and Linux caps live
// VMAs per process at `vm.max_map_count` (default 65530): left unmapped, a
// long-running server would trade a slow OOM kill for an abrupt
// `rootExit(70)` at roughly 65k live spawns, which is not this ticket's
// fix, only a different failure mode for the same unbounded growth.
@nosplit fn mapGuardedStack(bytes: int): int {
  if (bytes <= 0) {
    return 0
  }
  // `bytes + 2 * schedStackBytes`: one granule so an aligned base can always
  // be carved (as before), a second so a FULL granule is always available
  // below that base to dedicate as the guard, regardless of alignment
  // (#2433 — see header). Aligning to the granule even for an oversized
  // stack keeps one code path: masking will miss inside it either way (its
  // interior boundaries hold ordinary frame data, not the tag), and the
  // oversized-stack registry is what names it.
  let region = mapPages(bytes + 2 * schedStackBytes)
  if (region == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  // `alignUpStack(region + schedStackBytes)` rather than `+ 1`: forces base
  // at least one whole granule above region, so the fixed-width guard below
  // it (immediately below) is always entirely inside the mapping.
  let base = alignUpStack(region + schedStackBytes)
  if (!schedStackAligned(base) || base < region + schedStackBytes || base - region >= 2 * schedStackBytes) {
    return 0
  }
  // The head guard (#2433: floored to exactly one schedStackBytes granule,
  // PROT_NONE, not unmapped — see the header). A failure here is not fatal
  // — the stack above is intact and usable, it just has no guard — but it
  // is never expected, so it is not silent.
  if (!protectPages(base - schedStackBytes, schedStackBytes)) {
    return 0
  }
  // The unneeded leftover below the fixed guard (0 to schedStackBytes - 1
  // bytes, still alignment-dependent) is freed back — safe, since it sits
  // further from base than the guard, so a downward overflow always
  // reaches the guard first regardless of what reuses this sliver.
  let headSlop = base - schedStackBytes - region
  if (headSlop > 0) {
    if (!unmapPages(region, headSlop)) {
      return 0
    }
  }
  // The tail hole. See the header: the freed head sliver plus the fixed
  // head guard together take schedStackBytes of the mapping, so what
  // remains above base is bytes plus this leftover tail.
  let tailSlop = schedStackBytes - headSlop
  if (tailSlop > 0) {
    if (!unmapPages(base + bytes, tailSlop)) {
      return 0
    }
  }
  return base
}

// The worker OS-thread entry: unpack the {worker address, done-word address}
// block `schedStart` armed, drive green tasks until shutdown, then clear the
// done word so `joinWorker` observes the worker has left. TIMERS AND THE
// NETPOLLER ARE BOTH DRIVEN, and both must be. Same shape as `poolWorkerEntry`.
//
// A local `while` over the find/drain/step trio rather than `schedWorkerRun(w,
// parkMonoNs, pollDrain)`: the seam-carrying form takes function VALUES, and a
// function value is a `(code, env)` cell on the managed heap — which is exactly
// what `--freestanding` refuses to emit a descriptor for (§17.6, #1628). Every
// seam in this file is bound by DIRECT CALL for that reason.
//
// THE DRAIN IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS #1673. This loop used to be a
// bare `schedWorkerStepNow`, which probes the three queues and the timer list and
// NOTHING ELSE. So this — the only worker the runtime starts — never called
// `pollDrain`, and a green thread parked on the netpoller by `netAwaitReady` had
// no thread in the process that could ever wake it. It did not hang: the poll fd
// was never created either (see `boot` step 4b), so `netAwaitReady` bailed at its
// `pollFd == 0` guard, `netReadSock` fell through to the unscheduled-caller spin,
// exhausted it, and returned -1 — which `bit_rt_net_read` reports as an EMPTY
// READ, indistinguishable from an orderly peer close. Every socket read that had
// to wait for a packet silently returned "". Both halves are needed and neither
// is sufficient: a poll fd nothing drains parks the reader forever, and a drain
// with no poll fd returns 0 on its first line.
//
// THE ORDER IS `schedFindWorkIdle`'s, which is `runtime/sched/`'s: queues, then
// timers, then — only if all of those came up empty — the poller, whose woken
// tasks land on the global queue, which `schedWorkerStepAt` pops from when the
// drain reports a non-zero wake count. Polling
// only when idle is what keeps an `epoll_wait` syscall off the task-dispatch hot
// path; polling AFTER the timers is what stops a busy fd from starving a sleeper.
//
// `parkMonoNs()` IS THE POINT OF THE LOCAL LOOP (#1648). Timer deadlines are set
// by sibling ./time.bit's `bit_rt_time_sleep_ns` as `parkMonoNs() + ns`, so the
// worker that expires them must read the SAME clock. This used to call a
// `schedWorkerRunBare` that folded the clock away to a constant 0, against which
// no positive deadline is ever due — `println/sleep/println` printed its first
// line and hung forever, against a Bit-sourced `libbitrt.a`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE IDLE SLEEP (#1665) — kept identical in ../darwin/boot.bit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/sched`'s `schedWorkerStep` cannot reach a platform sleep for idle
// (that module may import neither park provider, and the providers' pinned
// `bit_rt_port_park_sleep_ns` is not a member of `libbitrt.a`, so `worker.bit`'s
// own comment states it is unreachable there — E0078, verified on both Linux
// triples). Its no-work branch instead burns `spinBackoff`, a busy spin, which
// is why an idle worker pegged a whole core.
//
// `addr == 0 && woke == 0` is EXACTLY `schedWorkerStep`'s own no-work predicate
// — `schedFindWorkAt` found nothing and `pollDrain` woke nothing — and this
// loop already computes both before calling into the step, so the idle case is
// distinguished HERE, by the caller, with no change to `runtime/sched`'s pinned
// `bit_rt_port_sched_worker_step_at` API. This is sound only because this
// runtime starts exactly ONE worker (ABI.md §5): `woke > 0` with nothing to pop
// off the global queue would mean another consumer raced this thread for it,
// which cannot happen here. When work IS found, `schedWorkerStepAt` is called
// exactly as before and runs it.
//
// The sleep is bracketed with `bit_rt_gc_blocking_begin`/`_end` (ABI.md §5),
// `runtime/sched/`'s own idle path and this file's `boot`/`joinWorker` sleeps: no
// frame here holds a live Bit reference while parked, so the collector may pass
// this worker's slot without waiting for a safepoint it will not reach until
// `parkSleepNs` returns. `scStopping` is checked first and by hand, matching
// `schedWorkerStep`'s own order, so a shutdown signalled while idle is still
// seen within one backoff step rather than after a sleep starts.
fn workerBody(arg: *byte): *byte {
  // ARM THIS THREAD'S CRASH DIAGNOSTIC FIRST (#2246, ./signal.bit): every
  // green task this worker will ever run executes below this line, so the
  // alternate signal stack must be live before any of them can fault.
  installAltStackForThisWorker()

  // CLAIM THE MUTATOR SLOT EAGERLY, BEFORE ANY BIT CODE RUNS (#1677/#1699).
  // Second only to arming the crash diagnostic above — installAltStackForThisWorker()
  // allocates nothing and touches no safepoint, so the window this call closes
  // is still shut. This is the thread's ENTRY function — the same place
  // `gcThreadExit` below runs at exit, and `runtime/sched/`'s `Worker.run` calls
  // `gc_hooks.threadEnter()` at the matching point. Lazy registration (claiming
  // a slot on first allocation or first safepoint poll) leaves a real window:
  // `schedWorkerStepAt`'s very first task dispatch can `schedSwitch` into a
  // green task body BEFORE this thread's own loop back edge is ever reached,
  // and #1677's reproducer proved a task that reads a live reference out of
  // shared state before its own first allocation/loop is invisible to the
  // rendezvous for that whole window — the object was swept 20/20 runs.
  // Calling `gcThreadEnter()` here closes it: this thread is a mutator before
  // it ever runs Bit code.
  gcThreadEnter()

  let block = taskAt(int(arg))
  let workerAddr = *block
  let doneAddr = *(block + 1)

  let w = taskAt(workerAddr)
  let s: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBlock)
  let backoff = parkMinBackoffNs
  // WORKER 0 POLLS, THE REST PARK (#1902). See `runtime/sched/sleep.bit` for the
  // argument; the short version is that leaving one worker on today's 1ms loop
  // keeps the netpoller and the timer queue driven with no new machinery, and
  // makes a wake this runtime forgets to send cost latency instead of a hang.
  let parks = *(w + wkId) != 0
  let n = 0
  while (n < WORKER_MAX_STEPS) {
    n = n + 1
    // ARM BEFORE LOOKING FOR WORK, never after: it is what closes the window
    // between a failed find-work and the sleep. A producer that publishes in
    // that window sees this worker armed, claims it, and the `parkWait` below
    // returns immediately instead of sleeping through the wake.
    if (parks) {
      schedIdleArm(w)
    }
    let addr = schedFindWorkAt(w, parkMonoNs(w + wkSleepReq))
    let woke = 0
    if (addr == 0) {
      woke = pollDrain(int(s), 0)
    }
    if (addr == 0 && woke == 0) {
      if (atomicLoad(s + scStopping) != 0) {
        break
      }
      gcBlockingBegin()
      if (parks) {
        schedIdlePark(s, w)
      } else {
        parkSleepNs(backoff, w + wkSleepReq)
      }
      gcBlockingEnd()
      backoff = parkNextBackoff(backoff)
      continue
    }
    // Running instead of parking: clear the arm so a producer does not spend
    // its one wake on a worker that is already busy.
    if (parks) {
      schedIdleDisarm(w)
    }
    backoff = schedWorkerStepAt(w, addr, woke, backoff)
    // TEARDOWN (#2167). `schedWorkerStep`, inside `schedWorkerStepAt`, already
    // unregistered the retired task from the collector's root set before
    // returning, so its stack and task block are unreachable and safe to
    // release here. `wkLastDone` — not `addr` — names the retired task,
    // because the netpoller-drain path picks its own task internally when
    // `addr == 0` (see `wkLastDone`'s own comment in `runtime/sched/worker.bit`).
    //
    // `mainTaskBlock` IS EXCLUDED, ALWAYS. It is a fixed module array
    // registered through `schedRegisterBigStack`, never `mapPages`'d, and it
    // reaches this identical `taskDone` transition through this identical
    // loop when `bit_main` returns. Unmapping it would `munmap` memory this
    // module never `mmap`'d.
    let doneTask = *(w + wkLastDone)
    if (doneTask != 0 && doneTask != int(ptrOf(mainTaskBlock))) {
      let dt = taskAt(doneTask)
      unmapPages(*(dt + taskStackBase), *(dt + taskStackBytes))
      unmapPages(doneTask, (taskWords + 1) * 8)
    }
    if (backoff == workerStepStop) {
      break
    }
  }

  // RELEASE THE MUTATOR SLOT (#1666). `runtime/sched/`'s `Worker.run` — the oracle
  // this function ports — carries `gc_hooks.threadEnter()` /
  // `defer gc_hooks.threadExit()`, and the exit half was missing here. The
  // worker registers lazily at its first safepoint poll and then died without
  // ever releasing slot 1, which stayed `mRunning` forever; from that moment
  // every rendezvous the boot thread started waited out `stwSpinBound` for a
  // thread that no longer existed and abandoned. `../../gc/gcworld.bit` states
  // the consequence on `gcThreadExit` itself: "a leaked slot stays `running`
  // forever, which corrupts nothing but makes every later rendezvous expire,
  // i.e. collection quietly stops." Measured at ~160 abandoned collections per
  // run regardless of workload size, and 0 with this call.
  //
  // PLACEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the oracle's: AFTER the run loop, so
  // no Bit code runs on this thread again, and BEFORE the done store, whose
  // tail is raw pointer arithmetic holding no managed reference. That is
  // `gcThreadExit`'s precondition. This is NOT the #1650/#1651 shape — nothing
  // is suppressed while the thread can still run Bit code; the slot is released
  // only once the thread is leaving. Releasing it any earlier would let a
  // rendezvous proceed past a thread that is still running, which is the defect
  // that sweeps live objects.
  gcThreadExit()

  let z: *i32 = nil
  atomicStore(z + doneAddr / 4, i32(0))
  return nil
}

// Trip bound for `joinWorker`, `runtime/thread`'s `JOIN_MAX_POLLS` unchanged:
// the join is finite by construction even if the deadline clock misbehaves, so
// a wedged worker fails the boot rather than hanging it (Power of 10, rule 2).
const joinMaxPolls: int = 100000

// `runtime/thread`'s `threadJoin` for THE one worker this runtime starts, with
// the platform clock and sleep called directly instead of taken as function
// values — this module must emit `--freestanding` and so cannot build one (see
// `workerBody`). Same contract: poll the done word against a monotonic deadline
// with bounded exponential backoff; true iff the worker finished in time.
fn joinWorker(done: *i32, timeoutNs: int): bool {
  // A non-positive timeout still gets one look at the word, so a join on an
  // already-finished worker succeeds rather than reporting a spurious timeout.
  if (atomicLoad(done) == i32(0)) {
    return true
  }
  let deadline = parkMonoNs(ptrOf(bootSleepReq)) + timeoutNs
  let backoff = parkMinBackoffNs
  let i = 0
  while (i < joinMaxPolls) {
    if (atomicLoad(done) == i32(0)) {
      return true
    }
    if (parkMonoNs(ptrOf(bootSleepReq)) >= deadline) {
      return false
    }
    parkSleepNs(backoff, ptrOf(bootSleepReq))
    backoff = parkNextBackoff(backoff)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

// The first green thread: run the user's `bit_main`, publish its exit code, then
// leave the task through `schedTaskExit`. `runtime/root/`'s `mainTrampoline`. Not
// `@nosplit` — `bit_main` is the whole user program and must reach safepoints.
fn mainTrampoline() {
  mainExitCode = int(bit_main())
  atomicStore(ptrOf(mainDone), i32(1))
  schedTaskExit()
}

